              {
                "target_type": "file",
                "file_path": "docs/pinned-todo-reactivation.md",
                "comment": "Design doc for pinned todos + reactivation via forkChat; why todo_pinning separate tool, why hash excludes pinned/reactivate"
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "sealed-session principle",
                "comment": "A session is never shared; after its process exits, its session JSON and triologue JSONL are sealed read-only. /load or --from reads them read-only and re-understands the transcript into a brand-new session (basis for branching)."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "checkReactivation",
                "comment": "COLLECT state function that evaluates done+pinned+reactivate candidates via a single forkChat (toolChoice='none', preserving prompt cache/retry), parses a JSON-array result, and reopens each via todo_update flipping done→false; runs before the nudge on the same 3-turn throttle."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "pinned todos & reactivation",
                "comment": "Pinned todos survive auto-clear at start of each turn; a pinned done-todo with a reactivate condition keeps hasOpenTodo() true; reactivation reopens a completed pinned todo when its condition becomes true, evaluated by the lead."
              },
              {
                "target_type": "term",
                "term_name": "team mode",
                "comment": "Not actually a mode but a state where teammates (child processes) are spawned to help the lead. Orthogonal to normal/plan (solo-normal, solo-plan, team-normal, team-plan). Architecture: Terminal -> Coordinator -> Lead -> Teammates."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "/mycc.md/webui-features",
            "text": "A Vue 3-based WebUI is served by mycc itself via an embedded Express + Vite + WebSocket stack (`src/serve/serve-hub.ts`, `src/web/`). It provides a browser-based chat interface as an alternative to the terminal REPL: Markdown rendering (letter-box → chat bubbles); interactive **cards** replacing terminal `ask()` prompts (input/confirm/choice) — `CardItem.vue`; session history via `/history` (backed by the durable triologue JSONL transcript, survives serve stop/restart and page closes); 30s disconnect-reconnect timer with graceful warm shutdown. For the developer reference (component layout, WS protocol, input/card bridges, reconnect replay), see `src/web/README.md`.\n\n**steering (WebUI mid-task direction):** lets the user queue direction for the agent *while the LLM is mid-run* without interrupting it. The steering queue lives in `ServeHub` as an **ephemeral, in-memory** buffer (`steeringQueue`, wiped on `stop()`), distinct from the file-backed **mail** system. `pushSteer`/`drainSteering`/`getSteeringNotes` are append/consume/peek. **Two consumption paths**: (1) **COLLECT** (`src/loop/states/collect.ts`, step 2c) — in-flight notes drained and injected verbatim as a `REMINDER` (no synthesis); (2) **PROMPT** (`src/loop/states/prompt.ts`) — if the run was interrupted (ESC/停止) and the user submits a fresh query, stale notes are merged with the query via a `forkChat` synthesis call (`synthesizeWithSteering()`), replacing the raw query; the queue is drained regardless of success and raw query is the fallback. The two paths are mutually exclusive (PROMPT drains first). Frontend (`src/web/src/main.ts`, `ChatInput.vue`, `SteeringBuffer.vue`): when `isRunning`, `send()` routes to `sendSteer`; the buffer bar is populated solely by the server's `steer-echo` broadcast (single source of truth — no local push); `steer-flush` clears it. `/history` returns `{ messages, steeringBuffer }` where `steeringBuffer` is a peek (survives refresh/reconnect within the same serve session; not a serve stop/restart).\n\n**file upload (WebUI chat-box attachments):** mirrors the steering design — same ephemeral-queue architecture in `ServeHub` (`fileUploadQueue`), same two consumption paths (COLLECT in-flight vs PROMPT stale). Key contrast: file uploads are **informational resources saved and noted, never synthesized** — no `forkChat` merge at PROMPT. Files travel inline in the JSON `WsMessage` as base64 (no multer/multipart); both `input` and `steer` carry an optional `files` array. COLLECT (step 2d) drains and saves to `./.mycc/uploaded/` as `${Date.now()}_${filename}` (base64-decoded), injecting a `REMINDER` listing each file (with truncated user-text preview if provided). PROMPT drains stale files with the same save logic and a `REMINDER` — no synthesis (files are read via `read_picture`/`read_file` in the next turn). No client-side buffer bar for files (`file-upload`/`file-flush` echoes are no-ops). The upload queue is not in `/history` and does not survive a serve stop/restart.\n\n**neglection and esc return:** neglection is triggered when the user presses ESC during LLM inference — signals the agent to quickly wrap up without further tool calls. \"Neglected mode\" aborts ongoing LLM calls, buffers subsequent output, forces a text-only response. Key: `agentIO.isNeglectedMode()`, `setNeglectedMode(value)`, `onNeglected(callback)`; IPC message `{ type: 'neglection' }` (coordinator → lead on ESC).\n\n**esc-aware wrap-up:** on ESC during LLM inference the system enters **wrap-up mode** — the LLM call is aborted via AbortController, a quick \"wrap-up\" LLM call runs in background to produce a concise text-only response, the prompt line reappears immediately, and the wrap-up result is displayed when ready (with commit/rollback logic). Grace period: if the user starts typing before wrap-up completes, the result is discarded. See `docs/esc-wrap-up-redesign.md`. `ctx.core.escAware(operation, onCleanUp)` wraps slow operations for ESC-aware quick return — on ESC the original promise continues in background, `onCleanUp` is invoked immediately, its result returned to the caller. `Core.escAware()` (parent) uses `agentIO.onNeglected()`; `ChildCore.escAware()` is a placeholder (TODO: IPC). Use `escAware` for high-level operations (tool execution, file ops, network requests); use a manual `AbortController` for low-level stream handling (`retryChat()`, hint round). Currently used by tool execution (`src/loop/states/tool.ts`).\n\n**crossroad:** improves LLM response quality by detecting \"turning words\" (e.g., \"However\", \"Wait\", \"但\", \"但是\") in the draft response, generating alternative continuations via parallel `forkChat` calls, selecting the best path while preserving prompt cache. Implementation: `src/hook/crossroad.ts` (runs in the HOOK state before tool execution; debug with `--debug-tp`). Consecutive crossroads contribute to the hint round confusion index.\n\n**@-prefix teammate label convention:** teammate tool output and status messages are routed to a separate `state.teammateMessages` array rather than the main chat log. Routing is decided by a string convention on the existing `label` field — no new WS message types, no `sender` field. The label is `@<teammate>/<tool>` (e.g., `@coder/bash`); `@` marks a teammate message, `/` splits teammate name from tool name. No tool tag → `@<teammate>`. Data flow: the tool tag was historically dropped at the IPC boundary, so **child** (`src/context/child/core.ts`, `ChildCore.brief()`) forwards `tool` in the `log` IPC payload, and **parent** (`src/context/parent/team.ts`, `handleChildMessage()`) builds `@${sender}/${tool}` (falls back to `@${sender}`). Other lifecycle paths (`teammate_ready`, `eta_update`, `status`, `exit`/`error`) are intentionally NOT prefixed. Frontend (`src/web/src/main.ts`): messages whose `label` starts with `@` go into `teammateMessages`; `/history` returns a flat array the client splits. Approximate persistence: teammate messages flow through the same `messageLog` (`MAX_LOG_SIZE = 1000`), replayed on reconnect, but lost on serve stop/restart (the transcript records teammate turns, not their brief/log output).\n\n**Teammate accordion UI:** teammate messages grouped by name and rendered in an accordion UI rather than mixed into the main chat. **`TeammateCard.vue`** — floating top-right card, one row per active teammate (`@name(count): currentTool`), click to open the drawer; hides when the drawer is open or no teammate messages. **`TeammateDrawer.vue`** — right-half panel with stacked accordions per teammate; header `@name(count): current_tool`, body is a flat chronological timeline with `[tool]` prefix tags; first accordion expanded by default; multiple may be open; ✕ closes. Grouping is a frontend computed property — no backend per-teammate state.",
            "title": "WebUI Features",
            "summary": "WebUI Features: mycc's Vue 3 browser chat (embedded Express+Vite+WS in src/serve/serve-hub.ts, SPA in src/web/). Markdown bubbles, interactive cards (CardItem.vue: input/choice/confirm/notice) replacing ask() prompts, durable /history (triologue JSONL), 30s disconnect/warm-shutdown. Main reference: src/web/README.md.\n\n**Steering:** ephemeral in-memory steeringQueue in ServeHub (pushSteer/drainSteering/getSteeringNotes/resolveSteering, wiped on stop) — two mutually exclusive paths: COLLECT (step 2c) drains in-flight notes as verbatim REMINDER; PROMPT synthesizes stale notes via forkChat (synthesizeWithSteering), raw query fallback. Frontend: send() routes to sendSteer mid-run, buffer bar fed solely by steer-echo broadcasts. Note: actual send() routes on isWaiting (input) vs else (steer), not exactly isRunning.\n\n**File upload:** mirrors steering (fileUploadQueue), inline base64 in WS payloads, saved to .mycc/uploaded/ as ${Date.now()}_${filename}, REMINDER only (never synthesized, no forkChat).\n\n**ESC:** neglection (agentIO.isNeglectedMode/setNeglectedMode/onNeglected, IPC 'neglection') + escAware wrap-up (aborts LLM via AbortController, background wrap-up call; Core.escAware uses onNeglected, ChildCore.escAware is placeholder/TODO). See docs/esc-wrap-up-redesign.md.\n\n**Crossroad** (src/loop/crossroad.ts): turning-word detection, parallel forkChat continuations, best-path selection.\n\n**@-prefix teammate labels:** @name/tool convention; child forwards tool in log IPC (ChildCore.brief), parent builds label (team.ts handleChildMessage); TeammateCard.vue/TeammateDrawer.vue accordion UI with frontend-only grouping.",
